{"ts": "2026-07-06T11:51:09+0530", "level": "INFO", "logger": "agent_framework", "trace_id": "0d96fed32ab44fe6c9e892e8458afaac", "span_id": "3971e0d73e1b0479", "parent_span_id": "-", "request_id": "AF82F358", "msg": "{'role': 'tool', 'parts': [{'type': 'tool_call_response', 'id': 'bf6854251', 'response': '{\\n  \"results\": [\\n    {\\n      \"source\": \"FAB_Credit_Pricing_Policy_v2.4\",\\n      \"clause\": \"2.4\",\\n      \"section\": \"2.4 Relationship Value\",\\n      \"effective_date\": \"\",\\n      \"stale\": false,\\n      \"score\": 0.0325,\\n      \"text\": \"## 2.4 Relationship Value\\\\n\\\\nFAB recognises that relationship profitability extends beyond the individual transaction. A relationship-adjusted pricing approach applies where a client demonstrates a material cross-sell contribution, measured as total annual revenue across all FAB products divided by total RWA. Relationship Value adjustments may reduce the required margin by up to 25 basis points from the standard floor, subject to:\\\\n\\\\n- Documented cross-sell revenues exceeding AED 500,000 per annum across the relationship.\\\\n- Approval by the relevant credit authority (see Section 5).\\\\n- Annual review of continued cross-sell performance.\\\\n\\\\n## 3. Minimum Pricing Floors by Product\\\\n\\\\nThe following tables set out the minimum all-in pricing floors applicable to each product category. These floors represent the absolute minimum below which no transaction may be executed without a formal Pricing Exception (Section 6). Floors are stated in basis points over the applicable FTP rate unless otherwise specified.\\\\n\\\\nNOTE: FTP rates are published monthly by FAB Treasury and are available on the internal Treasury portal. All floor rates in this section are stated as SPREADS over FTP. The total all-in rate must not fall below FTP + the stated floor spread.\\\\n\\\\n## 3.1 Corporate Term Loans\\\\n\\\\nTable 3.1 sets out minimum pricing floors for standard corporate term loans, segmented by client internal credit rating and facility tenor.\\\\n\\\\n| Client Rating   | Tenor ≤ 1 Year                                        | Tenor 1-3 Years   | Tenor 3-5 Years   | Tenor > 5 Years   | Notes                                          |\\\\n|-----------------|-------------------------------------------------------|-------------------|-------------------|-------------------|------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| AAA / AA        | 65 bps                                                | 80 bps            | 100 bps           | 130 bps           | Investment grade sovereign or quasi- sovereign |\\\\n| A               | 85 bps                                                | 105 bps           | 130 bps           | 165 bps           | Strong investment grade corporate              |\\\\n| BBB             | 115 bps                                               | 145 bps           | 180 bps           | 220 bps           | Standard investment grade                      |\\\\n| BB              | 165 bps                                               | 210 bps           | 260 bps           | 320 bps           | Sub-investment grade / leveraged               |\\\\n| B               | 240 bps                                               | 300 bps           | 370 bps           | 450 bps           | Speculative / requires senior credit approval  |\\\\n| CCC and below   | Not permitted without Group Credit Committee approval |                   |                   |                   | See Section 6                                  |\\\\n\\\\nEXAMPLE: A BBB-rated client requesting a 4-year AED term loan. FTP for 4-year AED = 4.85% (as at June 2024). Minimum floor spread = 180 bps. Minimum all-in rate = 4.85% + 1.80% = 6.65%.\\\\n\\\\n## 3.2 Revolving Credit Facilities (RCFs)\\\\n\\\\nRevolving credit facilities are subject to the pricing floors in Table 3.2. Commitment fees on undrawn portions are in addition to the drawn margin and are not included in the floor calculations below.\\\\n\\\\n| Client Rating   | Drawn Margin Floor   | Commitment Fee Floor   | Facility Fee Floor   | Tenure ≤ 3 Years   | Tenure > 3 Years   |\\\\n|-----------------|----------------------|------------------------|----------------------|--------------------|--------------------|\\\\n| AAA / AA        | 60 bps               | 20 bps                 | 10 bps               | Applies            | Add 15 bps         |\\\\n| A               | 80 bps               | 25 bps                 | 12 bps               | Applies            | Add 20 bps         |\\\\n| BBB             | 110 bps              | 30 bps                 | 15 bps               | Applies            | Add 25 bps         |\\\\n| BB              | 160 bps              | 40 bps                 | 20 bps               | Applies            | Add 40 bps         |\\\\n| B               | 220 bps              | 55 bps                 | 25 bps               | Applies            | Add 60 bps         |\\\\n\\\\n## 3.3 Syndicated Loans - FAB as Mandated Lead Arranger\\\\n\\\\nWhere FAB acts as Mandated Lead Arranger (MLA) or Bookrunner on a syndicated transaction, the following additional floors apply on top of the standard term loan floors in Table 3.1:\\\\n\\\\n| Fee Component     | Minimum Floor                     | Notes                         |\\\\n|-------------------|-----------------------------------|-------------------------------|\\\\n| Arrangement Fee   | 30 bps of facility amount         | Payable upfront by borrower   |\\\\n| Underwriting Fee  | 15 bps of underwritten portion    | On retained exposure          |\\\\n| Participation Fee | 10 bps                            | On sold-down portions         |\\\\n| Agency Fee        | USD 25,000 / AED 90,000 per annum | If FAB acts as Facility Agent |\\\\n\\\\n## 4. Funds Transfer Pricing (FTP) Framework\\\\n\\\\nFAB operates a matched-maturity FTP framework under which each credit facility is assigned an internal cost of funds (FTP rate) corresponding to its contractual tenor and repricing frequency. FTP rates are set monthly by FAB Treasury and published internally by the 5th business day of each month.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"source\": \"FAB_Credit_Pricing_Policy_v2.4\",\\n      \"clause\": \"4.1\",\\n      \"section\": \"4.1 FTP Rate Determination\",\\n      \"effective_date\": \"\",\\n      \"stale\": false,\\n      \"score\": 0.0323,\\n      \"text\": \"## 4.1 FTP Rate Determination\\\\n\\\\nThe FTP rate for each facility is determined as follows:\\\\n\\\\n1. Base Rate: The prevailing AED or relevant currency EIBOR (or applicable risk-free rate) for the matching tenor.\\\\n2. Liquidity Premium: An additional spread to reflect FAB\\'s cost of term funding in excess of overnight rates, set quarterly by Treasury.\\\\n3. Tenor Premium: A further adjustment for facilities with tenors exceeding 3 years, reflecting FAB\\'s increased balance sheet commitment.\\\\n\\\\n| Tenor Bucket   | EIBOR Tenor   | Liquidity Premium   | Tenor Premium   | Illustrative FTP (Jun 2024)   |\\\\n|----------------|---------------|---------------------|-----------------|-------------------------------|\\\\n| ≤ 3 months     | 3-month       | 15 bps              | 0 bps           | 5.25%                         |\\\\n| 3-12 months    | 6-month       | 20 bps              | 0 bps           | 5.40%                         |\\\\n| 1-3 years      | 12-month      | 30 bps              | 10 bps          | 5.55%                         |\\\\n| 3-5 years      | 12-month      | 35 bps              | 25 bps          | 5.85%                         |\\\\n| 5-7 years      | 12-month      | 40 bps              | 40 bps          | 6.05%                         |\\\\n| > 7 years      | 12-month      | 50 bps              | 60 bps          | 6.35%                         |\\\\n\\\\nIMPORTANT: FTP rates are indicative only. Relationship Managers must obtain confirmed FTP rates from Treasury at the time of term sheet issuance. Historical FTP rates are available on the Treasury pricing portal.\\\\n\\\\n## 5. Pricing Approval Authority Matrix\\\\n\\\\nAll credit pricing decisions must be approved at the appropriate level prior to issuance of a term sheet or credit sanction. The approval authority is determined by the combination of facility size, client credit rating, and the extent to which the proposed pricing deviates from the standard floor rates in Section 3.\\\\n\\\\n## 5.1 Standard Pricing - Within Floor Rates\\\\n\\\\n| Facility Size                | Client Rating   | Minimum Approval Authority            |\\\\n|------------------------------|-----------------|---------------------------------------|\\\\n| Up to AED 25 million         | All ratings     | Relationship Manager + Credit Analyst |\\\\n| AED 25M - 100M               | AAA to BBB      | Senior RM+Team Head                   |\\\\n| AED 25M - 100M               | BB and below    | Segment Credit Head                   |\\\\n| AED 100M - 500M              | AAA to BBB      | Segment Credit Head                   |\\\\n| AED 100M - 500M              | BB and below    | Regional Credit Committee (RCC)       |\\\\n| AED 500M - 1 billion         | All ratings     | Country Credit Committee (CCC)        |\\\\n| Above AED 1 billion          | All ratings     | Group Credit Committee (GCC)          |\\\\n| Any amount -Sovereign or GRE | All ratings     | Group Credit Committee (GCC)          |\\\\n\\\\n## 6. Pricing Exceptions\\\\n\\\\nA Pricing Exception applies when a proposed transaction is priced below the standard floors set out in Section 3, or where the calculated RAROC falls below the 13.5% hurdle rate. All Pricing Exceptions must be formally documented and approved before any term sheet is issued.\\\\n\\\\n## 6.1 Mandatory Documentation\\\\n\\\\nA Pricing Exception request must include the following:\\\\n\\\\n4. Full pricing model with all-in rate, RAROC calculation, and sensitivity analysis.\\\\n5. Written justification explaining the commercial rationale for the below-floor pricing.\\\\n6. Market evidence demonstrating that the proposed pricing is competitive.\\\\n7. Statement of relationship value, quantifying cross-sell revenues and total relationship RAROC.\\\\n8. Confirmation that the exception has been reviewed and endorsed by the Head of Credit Pricing.\\\\n\\\\n## 6.2 Approval Authority for Pricing Exceptions\\\\n\\\\n| Exception Type      | Max Deviation from Floor   | Approval Required              |\\\\n|---------------------|----------------------------|--------------------------------|\\\\n| Minor exception     | Up to 15 bps below floor   | Segment Credit Head            |\\\\n| Standard exception  | 15-40 bps below floor      | Country Credit Committee       |\\\\n| Major exception     | 40-80 bps below floor      | Group Credit Committee         |\\\\n| Strategic exception | Above 80 bps or RAROC < 8% | Chief Commercial Officer + CRO |\\\\n\\\\nWARNING: Any transaction that proceeds without the required Pricing Exception approval is a policy breach. Relationship Managers who execute transactions below floor without approval are subject to disciplinary action under FAB\\'s HR Policy (HR-POL-DISC-001).\\\\n\\\\n## 7. Concentration and Exposure Limits\\\\n\\\\n## 7.1 Single Obligor Limits\\\\n\\\\nThe following single-obligor credit concentration limits apply to the aggregation of all credit facilities across all FAB entities for a single counterparty or group of connected counterparties:\\\\n\\\\n| Counterparty Type                          | Maximum Exposure                                  | Notes                                           |\\\\n|--------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Non-bank corporate (Investment Grade)      | AED 3.5 billion                                   | Per group of connected counterparties           |\\\\n| Non-bank corporate (Sub- Investment Grade) | AED 1.2 billion                                   | Per group; requires GCC approval above AED 800M |\\\\n| Financial Institution (Investment Grade)   | AED 2.5 billion                                   | Interbank and trade lines combined              |\\\\n| Government Entity (GRE)                    | AED 5.0 billion                                   | UAE-domiciled GREs only; foreign GREs: AED 2.0B |\\\\n| Sovereign                                  | Country limit as approved by Board Risk Committee | Separate sovereign risk framework applies       |\\\\n| Real Estate Sector (combined)              | 25% of total corporate portfolio                  | Quarterly monitoring by Risk Management         |\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \"total_results\": 2,\\n  \"freshness_warning\": false,\\n  \"latency_ms\": 243585.45\\n}'}]}"}
